  "background": "Acts opens by referring back to Luke’s “first book” and addressing Theophilus again, making Luke–Acts a two-volume narrative. The author presents selected events, speeches, journeys, trials, and summaries to show the risen Jesus continuing His work through the Spirit and His witnesses. The speeches are part of Luke’s literary presentation of apostolic preaching; Acts is historically interested and theologically shaped, not a stenographic transcript or an exhaustive manual for reproducing every event.\n\nThe earliest Jesus movement in Acts is thoroughly Jewish, rooted in Israel’s Scriptures, temple life, synagogue debate, and messianic hope. As Samaritans and Gentiles are welcomed, difficult questions about identity and fellowship become central. Internal Jewish disputes must never be turned into antisemitism, and Roman power, local elites, crowds, and economic interests all contribute to conflict. Acts repeatedly requires readers to distinguish what the narrative describes from what Scripture commands for every church.",
