      "context": "The “Hellenists” and “Hebrews” in Acts 6 are both Jewish groups, distinguished largely by language and cultural background. The neglected widows therefore reveal an inequity inside the church rather than a simple Jew-versus-Gentile problem. The community responds by sharing responsibility with seven trusted men, several bearing Greek names, while the apostles remain focused on prayer and the ministry of the word.\n\nStephen’s speech retells Israel’s story in a sharp prophetic pattern of resistance, and both Stephen and his opponents are Jewish. The speech must not be converted into a Christian verdict against Jewish people. Acts 8 then moves through Samaria to an Ethiopian court official who is also a eunuch. Ancient eunuch status involved social and bodily realities unlike modern identity categories, so interpreters should neither erase the person’s embodied difference nor force present-day labels the text does not supply.",
